Concerning the intended GPU hardware, I would stick to an open/mp
porting, since this would avoid the dependence on just one vendor
(NVIDIA, for example).  Probably this choice is not the best one for
optimizing performance, but I do not want the code to depend
critically on external s/w and exclusive hardware. ORAC is designed to
accumulate simulation times in multiple simulations launched in
parallel on the MPI level with a simple input file.  On the strong
scaling OpenMP layer (force routines), ORAC scales poorly up to a
factor of only 4 on an Intel7700 8-cores CPU.  I have on my local
workstation for testing an nvidia GeForce GTX 1050 Ti.  Gaining a
factor of 7-10 for the strong scaling layer will already be a good
objective. A global gain of e.g. 10 using GeForce GTX 1050 Ti would
make the code maybe two or three times slower than glomacs, which is
not at all bad for me. Some inefficient old-fashioned routines must be
simply replaced by modern alternative. These include the constraints
routine and the neighbor list.  ORAC implements a r-RESPA multiple
time step algorithm for the non bonded force that should be
poreserved. ORAC works, in general, on 3-fold nonbonded splitting with
r-RESPA. Long range forces [7,cutoff], intermediate [5,7] and
short-range [0-5]. The LR force routine receives as input the neighbor
list, computes the LR forces (to be integrated less often) and sets up
the neighbor list for the intermediate range. The IR layer receives as
input the neighbor list computed by the LR layer, computes the IR
forces, and produces the inner neighbor list for the SR layer. This
design, unlike other part of the OpenMP code (constraints, bonded,
neighbor list and Ewald), scales pretty well (up to 10-12 speed up)
with OpenMP and should be preserved. Nothing, however, prevents
adopting a drastic reorganization of the force routine, e.g. by
avoiding the identification in the double loop of contacts to be
excluded (e.g. those involving atoms in a bond in a bending or
torsion). These interactions could be computed in the double loop and
subtracted afterward. This approach would actually simplify the
routine and make it more amenable to GPU porting. Also the switching
functions for the non-bonded shell can be optimized using
multiplicatione quasi-Heaviside functions

The outer neighbor list (the one received by the LongRange layer of the
force routine) must probably be rewritten. Now orac can use a standard
spherical NL and, for large samples, a linked-cell NL both written
ages ago. To reduce the cost of these calculation, ORAC NL is based on
*groups* with an average of 3.4 atoms. This reduces the cost for both
variants by a factor of 10.

The Ewald implementation is another fundamental aspect to be
considered in the porting. ORAC was implemented with a public FFT
routine provided by Thomas Darden 30 years ago. This is a Fortran 66
serial code that needs apropriate options to compile. The OpenMP
version, however, runs exclusively using the FFTW3 FFTE3/dev library,
which has a rather inefficient OpenMP version. When running on NVIDIA
hardware, it would be nice to have the code interfaced also to the
cuda-ffts.
The bonded part (which include bond, bend, totsions and direct space Ewald correction terms) is plagued by  race conditions and mus be rewritten

